 signature, and plays back with `playdemo`. Not verified: co-op.
 
+**Queued rendering stays on (the render lock).** With `mat_queue_mode 2` the main thread fills one of two
+queued render contexts while a job on the thread pool plays the other back, and which one is the main
+thread's lives in a thread-local pointer outside the snapshot. A restore that left it alone crashed in the
+render calls a moment later, so `mat_queue_mode` used to be forced to 0 around every operation (a stutter
+each way, and a third of the frame rate for a tool that kept it off). Now a save and a restore run inside
+`IMaterialSystem::Lock()` (`VMaterialSystem080`, vtable slots in `sdk.h` `matslot`, checked at init): it
+waits for the render job, takes the material system's mutex and gives the thread the device's context.
+The save keeps what `Lock()` returned and a restore calls `Unlock()` with the value of *that save*, since
+the memory it brought back is the locked state of that moment. `snap_opt render_lock 0` is the old
+behaviour (a snapshot taken that way can only be restored that way). Tested with -vulkan: 60-80 restores
+in a row in sp_a2_laser_stairs and mp_coop_laser_2 (the maps that crashed) and level changes after them.
+
+**Exports for plugins that drive snapshots** (`src/plugin.cpp`): `ChocoRestoresDeviceState()` (1 when a restore sets the Direct3D device back too) and `ChocoGetSnapshotStatus(ChocoSnapshotStatus*)`: counts of saves done, restores done and requests failed (`control::Done()`; every request ends up in exactly one of them, also one refused at the command or dropped by a level change), and whether queued rendering stays on. A plugin compares the counts with what they were when it sent `snap_save` / `snap_load`, instead of waiting a number of frames.
